@meta
id: 32-12
title: Refactor — the resolver is doing too much
chapter: 32
type: scenario
skills: multi-file-refactor, register-named, op-linewise, global-delete
par: 128
minutes: 10
vscode: cut in one tab, paste in another, lose it if you copy twice
open: src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts src/domain/order/order.provider.ts src/domain/order/order.entity.ts src/domain/order/order.schema.graphql
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
    ORD-990  `cancelOrder` decides whether an order may be cancelled.
             That decision belongs in the provider: the consumer
             cancels orders too and does not go through the resolver,
             so a shipped order can be cancelled from the queue today.

             Move the guard down a layer. The resolver maps GraphQL to
             a provider call and does nothing else.

After the move:

  - order.resolver.ts — `cancelOrder` is one line, a call to
    `this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id)`. The `exception` import goes
    with the guard; nothing in the file throws any more.

  - order.provider.ts — `cancel` looks the order up and refuses a
    shipped one before it updates anything. It has `findOne` already,
    and it takes an `id`, not an `args` object, so the line you move
    needs adjusting as you land it.

The entity and the schema do not change. The public API is identical
before and after — that is what makes this a refactor.

@keys
| `"a6dd` | delete six lines into register a |
| `"ap` | put register a below this line |
| `dw` `x` | delete a word, then the punctuation after it |
| `:g/pat/d` | delete the line that matches |

@hints
Moving code between files is a delete into a named register and a put.
Use a named one: everything else you delete on the way would overwrite
the unnamed register, and you would find out at the paste.
--
In the resolver the guard is six lines — the lookup, the blank line, the
three lines of `if`, and the blank line after it. Taking the blank lines
with it is what makes the paste land correctly spaced at the other end.
--
`/const order = await` `Enter` `"a6dd`, then `:g/^import { exception/d`.
In the provider, `/public async cancel` `Enter` `"ap` puts the block
straight under the signature, and then two small edits turn
`this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id)` into `this.findOne(id)`.

@solution
`/const order = await` `Enter` `"a6dd` `:g/^import { exception/d`
`:bn` `/public async cancel` `Enter` `"ap`
`/orderProvider` `Enter` `dwx` `/args` `Enter` `dwx` `:wqa`.
111 keystrokes.

**A named register is the whole technique.** Cross-file moves are one of
the few places where the unnamed register will genuinely bite you: you
delete the block, then delete the now-unused import on your way out, and
the import is what you paste. `"a` costs two keystrokes and removes the
failure mode entirely. Get in the habit of reaching for a letter whenever
the yank and the put are more than a couple of keystrokes apart.

**Take the blank lines with the block.** Six lines, not four. The two
blank lines are part of the shape of the code, and if you carry them the
paste needs no tidying at either end. Carrying structure rather than text
is the same instinct as `dap` over `4dd`.

**Adjust at the destination, not the source.** The moved line refers to
`this.orderProvider` and `args.id`, neither of which exists in the
provider. Two `dw` `x` pairs fix both, in place, after the paste. Editing
it before the move would mean editing code that is briefly wrong in a
file where it does not belong.

One thing that is not a keystroke: this ticket is worth doing because of
the consumer, not because of the layering. "Business logic belongs in the
provider" is a style rule and reasonable people argue about it. "A
shipped order can be cancelled from the queue today" is a bug, and it is
the sentence that gets the refactor reviewed and merged.

@start src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    const order = await this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

@injectable()
export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(@inject(OrderProvider) protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async order(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.findOne(args.id);
  }

  public async cancelOrder(_: unknown, args: { id: string }) {
    return this.orderProvider.cancel(args.id);
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status === 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start src/domain/order/order.schema.graphql
type Query {
  order(id: ID!): Order
}

type Mutation {
  cancelOrder(id: ID!): Order
}
